 "drivingQuestion": "What does 'for your love is better than wine' mean?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "avodah_zarah.35a.6",
   "daf": "35a",
   "sentenceId": 5,
   "he": "הִשִּׂיאוֹ לְדָבָר אַחֵר וְכוּ׳. מַאי ״כִּי טוֹבִים דֹּדֶיךָ מִיָּיִן״? כִּי אֲתָא רַב דִּימִי אָמַר: אָמְרָה כְּנֶסֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל לִפְנֵי הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא: רִבּוֹנוֹ שֶׁל עוֹלָם, עֲרֵיבִים עָלַי דִּבְרֵי דוֹדֶיךָ יוֹתֵר מִיֵּינָהּ שֶׁל תּוֹרָה.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that Rabbi Yehoshua «diverted» Rabbi Yishmael’s attention «to another matter,» and began discussing the verse: “For your love is better than wine” (Song of Songs 1:2). The Gemara asks: «What» is the meaning of the verse: «“For your love [dodekha] is better than wine”? When Rav Dimi came» from Eretz Yisrael to Babylonia, he «said: The congregation of Israel said before the Holy One, Blessed be He: Master of the Universe, the statements of Your beloved ones [dodekha],» i.e., the Sages, «are more pleasant to me than the wine of» the written «Torah» itself."
  }
 ],
